---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/content-pages.css';
import '../../styles/bench-methodology.css';
import { ARENAS, activeLevels, canonicalDenominator, operationalWeight } from '../../data/benchmarkSpec';

const visualLevels = activeLevels(ARENAS.visual);
const fullWeight = canonicalDenominator(ARENAS.visual);
const activeWeight = operationalWeight(ARENAS.visual);
const protocols = [
    { number: 1, name: 'Mole', anchor: 'level-1-mole' },
    { number: 2, name: 'Rhino', anchor: 'level-2-rhino' },
    { number: 3, name: 'Chimpanzee', anchor: 'level-3-chimpanzee' },
    { number: 4, name: 'Owl', anchor: 'level-4-owl' },
    { number: 5, name: 'Eagle', anchor: 'level-5-eagle' },
];
---

<Layout title="Visual Bench Methodology | Ultimate Bench" description="Administration, scoring, security, interpretation, and limitations for Visual Bench.">
<main class="content-page bench-methodology visual-methodology" data-visual-methodology data-active-level-count={visualLevels.length} data-complete-weight={fullWeight}>
    <header class="page-heading" id="overview">
        <span class="page-kicker">Arena 01 · Methodology</span>
        <h1>Visual Bench Methodology</h1>
        <p>Visual Bench evaluates repeatable character recognition, visual localization, spatial binding, and midpoint reasoning across fixed visual task conditions of increasing complexity.</p>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-navigation" aria-label="Visual Bench methodology sections">
        <a href="#construct">What it measures</a><a href="#administration">Administration</a><a href="#confidentiality">Confidentiality</a>
        <a href="#scoring">Scoring</a><a href="#status-handling">Statuses</a><a href="#weighting">Weighting</a>
        {protocols.map(protocol => <a href={`#${protocol.anchor}`}>Level {protocol.number} — {protocol.name}</a>)}
        <a href="#level-6-beholder" class="pending">Level 6 — Beholder (Planned)</a>
    </nav>

    <article class="content-card shared-methodology">
        <section id="construct" aria-labelledby="construct-title">
            <p class="section-label">Scope</p><h2 id="construct-title">What Visual Bench measures</h2>
            <p>The five Active levels use character images and fixed questions to test repeatable correctness under increasingly complex task conditions. Depending on the level, success can require recognizing depicted characters, knowing or recovering their names, locating them in a structured image, following coordinate conventions, locating named anchors, finding a midpoint, and binding each answer to the right question.</p>
            <p>Occlusion, segmentation artifacts, irregular cropping, color inversion, visual density, and appropriate use of permitted interface tools can also affect performance. No level isolates a single pure cognitive faculty. Visual Bench is not a comprehensive measure of general visual intelligence, every form of visual-spatial ability, or the underlying foundation model in isolation.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="administration" aria-labelledby="administration-title">
            <p class="section-label">Shared procedure</p><h2 id="administration-title">Public administration protocol</h2>
            <div class="procedure-grid">
                <div><strong>10 fixed questions</strong><span>presented together in one administration</span></div>
                <div><strong>10 administrations</strong><span>each in a new, isolated conversation</span></div>
                <div><strong>100 response instances</strong><span>binary scored; not 100 independent items</span></div>
            </div>
            <ol>
                <li>The level’s fixed active image and ten fixed questions are presented together through a publicly accessible chatbot product or official interactive model interface.</li>
                <li>Each repetition begins in a fresh, isolated conversation. Previous runs, answers, and accumulated conversation context are never supplied.</li>
                <li>The exact named product, model/configuration, and interface configuration are recorded; the administration date is recorded where the results system supports it.</li>
                <li>Web search is permitted whenever the evaluated interface makes it available. Code execution is also permitted when offered.</li>
                <li>Google AI Studio configurations with and without code execution are named separately, and AI Studio temperature is set to <strong>1</strong>. Other products may not expose equivalent controls.</li>
                <li>The documented answers and status rules produce one canonical level result.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><strong>The evaluated object is the accessible product configuration.</strong><p>Evaluations use ChatGPT, the Gemini browser chatbot, Claude’s chatbot, the standalone Grok chatbot (not Grok within X/Twitter), and Google AI Studio. Results describe the named chatbot product, model configuration, or accessible interface configuration—not an abstract raw foundation model tested through identical APIs.</p></div>
            <h3>Standardized and provider-controlled conditions</h3>
            <p>The evaluator standardizes the fixed stimulus and questions, ten-at-once presentation, fresh-conversation rule, repetition count, scoring, and recorded interface label. This is not a strictly closed-book or unaided-perception test: available search and code tools are part of practical product performance.</p>
            <p>Consumer interfaces may control sampling behavior, reasoning allocation, internal routing, image resizing, compression and preprocessing, tool invocation, caching, hidden system instructions, safety behavior, timeouts, and model snapshot selection. When a setting is not exposed, it cannot be made identical across providers and is treated as part of the tested product environment. Visual Bench prioritizes consistent evaluation of accessible products under documented interface conditions rather than claiming equal APIs, budgets, latency, hidden reasoning effort, or provider-side image processing.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="confidentiality" aria-labelledby="confidentiality-title">
            <p class="section-label">Benchmark security</p><h2 id="confidentiality-title">Active-stimulus confidentiality</h2>
            <p>The structure, administration procedure, scoring rules, configurations, and limitations of Visual Bench are documented publicly. While a benchmark version remains active, its images, complete character arrangements, exact questions, answer keys, item-level spatial layouts, and transformation details precise enough to reconstruct the stimuli remain confidential.</p>
            <p>This separation reduces contamination, targeted prompt preparation, model-specific optimization and benchmaxxing, while preserving fixed active materials for future evaluations. Methodological transparency does not require public release of active test contents. No claim of independent stimulus audit is made.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="scoring" aria-labelledby="scoring-title">
            <p class="section-label">Level results</p><h2 id="scoring-title">Scoring and repeated-response interpretation</h2>
            <p>Every Active level contains ten unique questions repeated across ten isolated administrations. Each correct, identifiable answer earns one point; an incorrect, missing, or non-matchable answer earns zero. The 0–100 result is the sum of those 100 binary-scored response instances.</p>
            <div class="callout numeric-example"><strong>What “84%” means</strong><p>84 correct responses out of 100 response instances generated from 10 fixed questions across 10 isolated administrations—not 84 of 100 independent test items.</p></div>
            <p>Repetition measures repeatable correctness and generation-to-generation stability on those fixed questions. Consistently correct answers score highly, alternating correct and incorrect answers receive partial credit, and consistently incorrect answers score zero. The result does not estimate performance over every possible image, character, coordinate, anchor pair, grid, prompt, or visual task. Large differences can be informative on this benchmark; small differences are not automatically definitive evidence of universal superiority.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="answer-adjudication" aria-labelledby="answer-adjudication-title">
            <p class="section-label">Manual review</p><h2 id="answer-adjudication-title">Answer adjudication</h2>
            <p>Historical scoring uses semantic identifiability rather than exact string identity. A response must identify the intended character unambiguously and remain reliably associated with its question.</p>
            <dl class="policy-list">
                <div><dt>Accepted when unambiguous</dt><dd>Full names, surnames, established aliases, translated names or alternate romanizations; differences in capitalization or punctuation; minor spelling errors; and extra commentary that does not contradict the answer. Formatting alone does not invalidate an identifiable, clearly mapped answer.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Not accepted by default</dt><dd>An actor’s name instead of the character, or a merely descriptive answer, unless it unambiguously identifies the intended character under the established historical adjudication.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Incorrect</dt><dd>Unrelated or contradictory answers, multiple proposed identities without one clear selection, and answers too ambiguous to identify the intended character.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Potentially non-evaluable</dt><dd>Output that cannot be mapped reliably to the ten questions. This is handled under the INVALID rule rather than silently guessed.</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="status-handling" aria-labelledby="status-title">
            <p class="section-label">Canonical semantics</p><h2 id="status-title">Status handling</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Composite treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">0</th><td>A completed, evaluable level with no correct response instances.</td><td>Zero points; the level’s full weight remains.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">INVALID</th><td>Three consecutive administrations could not be evaluated because the requested answers were absent, refused, unrelated, severely malformed, or not reliably mappable. Incorrect but evaluable answers do not trigger INVALID.</td><td>Zero points; the level’s full weight remains. It is distinct from a completed zero.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">UNAVAILABLE</th><td>The configuration could not be accessed for that Active level.</td><td>Zero numerator contribution; denominator remains 63; Active-level coverage is incomplete.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">NOT_TESTED</th><td>The configuration was not administered at that Active level.</td><td>Zero numerator contribution; denominator remains 63; Active-level coverage is incomplete.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Planned</th><td>A future level without an active protocol.</td><td>Excluded from scoring, coverage, and rank eligibility.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table></div>
            <p>After a third consecutive non-evaluable administration, testing stops and the level is stored as INVALID. The published repository does not establish a separate rule for retaining partial points from earlier runs, so this page does not claim one; the canonical aggregate receives the stored INVALID status and treats it as zero.</p>
        </section>
    </article>

    <section class="protocols" aria-label="Active level protocols">
        <article id="level-1-mole" class="content-card protocol" data-visual-protocol="1" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="level-1-mole-title">
            <header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 1 · weight 1</p><h2 id="level-1-mole-title">Mole</h2><p>Direct coordinate localization in an intact grid.</p></header>
            <section><h3>Purpose and stimulus</h3><p>Mole provides the baseline fixed-image condition: an intact 10 × 10 character grid with numerical row and column labels. Active contents and layouts are confidential.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Task</h3><p>For each of ten fixed coordinate queries, the model follows the grid convention, locates the requested cell, recognizes its character, names it, and preserves question-answer mapping.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Administration and scoring</h3><p>The same image and ten questions are presented together in ten fresh, isolated conversations, with no previous responses supplied: 10 unique questions × 10 administrations = 100 binary-scored response instances. Correct identifiable answers earn one point; incorrect, missing, or non-matchable answers earn zero. Maximum: 100. The shared three-consecutive non-evaluable rule produces INVALID.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Mole measures repeatable coordinate-based character localization on one fixed stimulus. It combines character familiarity and naming with coordinate following, localization, and answer binding. Its fixed grid, ten repeated questions, possible aliases, and manual adjudication bound generalization.</p></section>
        </article>

        <article id="level-2-rhino" class="content-card protocol" data-visual-protocol="2" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="level-2-rhino-title">
            <header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 2 · weight 2</p><h2 id="level-2-rhino-title">Rhino</h2><p>Coordinate localization after systematic lateral information loss.</p></header>
            <section><h3>Purpose and stimulus</h3><p>Rhino transforms the Mole image by removing the same lateral half of every column and concatenating the retained strips. It changes lateral visual information while preserving the underlying logical grid, coordinate convention, and ten questions. Active contents and layouts are confidential.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Task</h3><p>The model answers the same ten coordinate queries, now recognizing and locating characters from systematically partial visual evidence.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Administration and scoring</h3><p>The fixed Rhino image and questions are presented together in ten fresh, isolated conversations with no cross-run context, yielding 100 response instances from ten unique questions. Binary scoring and the shared INVALID rule are identical to Mole; maximum: 100.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>The Mole–Rhino comparison holds the logical grid and questions fixed while changing lateral visual information, but it does not isolate a pure faculty: crop effects vary by character and may interact with familiarity, naming, localization, and binding. One fixed transformed image and repeated questions limit generalization.</p></section>
        </article>

        <article id="level-3-chimpanzee" class="content-card protocol" data-visual-protocol="3" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="level-3-chimpanzee-title">
            <header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 3 · weight 4</p><h2 id="level-3-chimpanzee-title">Chimpanzee</h2><p>Coordinate localization under compound visual degradation.</p></header>
            <section><h3>Purpose and stimulus</h3><p>Chimpanzee applies an imperfect automatic background-removal process to the complete Rhino image after its lateral reduction. The result combines Rhino’s information loss with segmentation artifacts and further lost or altered visual evidence. The logical grid and ten coordinate questions remain fixed; active contents and transformation specifics are confidential.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Task</h3><p>The model answers the same coordinate queries while coping with the compound transformed image and maintaining character, location, and answer bindings.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Administration and scoring</h3><p>The fixed image and ten questions are presented together across ten fresh, isolated conversations without earlier responses, producing 100 binary-scored response instances. The shared scoring and INVALID rules apply; maximum: 100.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Chimpanzee measures repeatable localization under the full compound condition; it does not isolate segmentation robustness. The transformation does not necessarily make every queried cell uniformly harder. Character-specific artifact severity, familiarity, aliases, one fixed image, and repeated questions limit interpretation. Increasing complexity is a design property, not a claim of strictly monotonic empirical difficulty.</p></section>
        </article>

        <article id="level-4-owl" class="content-card protocol" data-visual-protocol="4" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="level-4-owl-title">
            <header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 4 · weight 8</p><h2 id="level-4-owl-title">Owl</h2><p>Anchor-based horizontal and vertical midpoint localization.</p></header>
            <section><h3>Purpose and stimulus</h3><p>Owl uses a separate 17 × 11 character grid containing a central image that spans a 5 × 3 area. The active image, exact arrangement, questions, anchor pairs, and answers are confidential.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Task</h3><p>Each question has the conceptual form “What character is between [Character X] and [Character Y]?” The two named anchors define a unique horizontal or vertical midpoint occupied by one identifiable target. Owl does not use diagonal midpoint relationships; those first appear in Eagle.</p><ol><li>Identify the first named anchor.</li><li>Identify the second named anchor.</li><li>Determine their spatial relationship.</li><li>Locate the unique character exactly between them.</li><li>Name that midpoint character.</li><li>Associate the answer with the correct question.</li></ol></section>
            <section><h3>Administration and scoring</h3><p>The same Owl image and ten fixed questions are presented together ten times. Every administration starts in a fresh, isolated conversation; prior responses are not supplied. Thus 10 unique questions × 10 administrations = 100 binary-scored response instances. A correct identifiable midpoint character earns one point; an incorrect, missing, or non-matchable answer earns zero. Maximum: 100. The shared three-consecutive non-evaluable rule produces INVALID.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Owl is not merely character recognition: it combines two-anchor search, alignment reasoning, unique-midpoint selection, target recognition, and binding across ten simultaneous questions. It remains dependent on character familiarity and naming, so it is not a pure measure of abstract spatial reasoning. Boundaries include one fixed stimulus, ten repeated questions rather than independently sampled items, alias ambiguity, possible effects from the central spanning image, and compound recognition/search/reasoning/binding demands.</p></section>
        </article>

        <article id="level-5-eagle" class="content-card protocol" data-visual-protocol="5" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="level-5-eagle-title">
            <header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 5 · weight 16</p><h2 id="level-5-eagle-title">Eagle</h2><p>A compound relational-localization stress test.</p></header>
            <section><h3>Purpose and stimulus</h3><p>Eagle combines a larger, denser visual field; mixed characters from unrelated fictional sources; animation and live action; irregular cropping; and selective local color inversion. Its active dimensions, production details, arrangement, questions, and answers remain confidential where disclosure could aid reconstruction.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Task</h3><p>As in Owl, the model locates two named anchors, determines their relationship, identifies and names the unique midpoint character, and preserves answer binding. Eagle includes horizontal, vertical, and diagonal midpoint relationships. It therefore combines recognition, search, localization, midpoint reasoning, and response management under heterogeneous corruption.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Administration and scoring</h3><p>The same active Eagle image and ten fixed questions are presented together in ten fresh, isolated conversations without prior responses, creating 100 response instances from ten unique questions. The shared binary scoring and INVALID rules apply; maximum: 100.</p></section>
            <section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Eagle measures repeatable correctness under the complete compound condition. It changes several conditions simultaneously and is not a controlled extension of Owl; an Owl–Eagle difference cannot isolate diagonal reasoning, cropping, inversion, density, search, or source familiarity. One fixed image, repeated questions, uneven character familiarity or corruption severity, naming ambiguity, and manual adjudication bound interpretation.</p></section>
        </article>
    </section>

    <article class="content-card closing-methodology">
        <section id="weighting" aria-labelledby="weighting-title">
            <p class="section-label">Official index</p><h2 id="weighting-title">Arena weighting and aggregate score</h2><p><strong>Fixed six-level normalization:</strong> the full ladder denominator is permanently 63. Operational weight is {activeWeight}/63, so the current maximum is 49.21%. Beholder reserves 32 points but has no model-level result. Future activation creates earning opportunity without changing the denominator.</p>
            <div class="table-wrap"><table data-visual-weights><thead><tr><th>Active level</th><th>Weight</th><th>Share of complete score</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                {visualLevels.map(level => <tr data-level={level.number} data-weight={level.weight}><th scope="row">{level.number} — {level.name}</th><td>{level.weight}</td><td>{level.weight}/63</td></tr>)}
                <tr><th scope="row">6 — Beholder (Planned)</th><td>32 reserved</td><td>32/63</td></tr><tr class="total"><th scope="row">Full ladder denominator</th><td>{fullWeight}</td><td>Permanent</td></tr>
            </tbody></table></div>
            <p><code>Visual score = (Mole + 2×Rhino + 4×Chimpanzee + 8×Owl + 16×Eagle) / 63</code></p>
            <p>Later levels contain more complex task conditions, and the official index intentionally gives success under those conditions greater importance. Eagle contributes 16/63 and Owl 8/63 of the permanent complete six-level Visual ladder. Among the currently operational Visual weight of 31, those levels represent 16/31 and 8/31 respectively. Equal weighting would also be an editorial choice, not an objectively neutral law. Powers of two are transparent, deterministic, monotonic, and easy to reproduce, but these are <strong>editorial importance weights</strong>, not empirically estimated psychometric difficulty parameters.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="coverage-ranking" aria-labelledby="coverage-title">
            <p class="section-label">Leaderboard</p><h2 id="coverage-title">Coverage, provisional results, and ranking</h2>
            <p>Complete Visual coverage means all five Active level results are included. Numeric zero and INVALID both retain coverage and weight. UNAVAILABLE and NOT_TESTED earn zero without reducing denominator 63, and make the score provisional and ineligible for an official arena rank. Missing Active result fields are data errors rather than statuses.</p>
            <p>Official Visual ranks include only complete-coverage results. Overall qualification requires rank-eligible results in all three arenas. Equal unrounded canonical scores receive the same competition rank and the next rank is skipped. Canonical calculations retain full precision; leaderboard composite displays round to one decimal place. Per-level integer percentages are already exact response counts out of 100.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="interpretation-limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-title">
            <p class="section-label">Boundaries</p><h2 id="limitations-title">Interpretation and limitations</h2>
            <p>Visual Bench supports comparisons of repeatable correctness on its documented fixed conditions for the named accessible product configurations. It can reveal large performance differences, instability across repetitions, and sensitivity across related or compound conditions.</p>
            <p>It does not establish psychometric validity, general visual intelligence, universal product superiority, causal explanations for errors, or performance over the full population of possible visual tasks. Fixed confidential stimuli reduce contamination but do not eliminate it. Character familiarity, naming conventions, manual adjudication, provider changes, permitted tools, and uncontrolled interface behavior can influence results.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="versioning-audit" aria-labelledby="audit-title">
            <p class="section-label">Auditability</p><h2 id="audit-title">Versioning and internal audit records</h2>
            <p>Public results preserve the named configuration, level result/status, and release or test date where the current data supports it. Internal evaluation records should additionally preserve the benchmark and stimulus version, private stimulus identifier, interface, configurable temperature, web and code-execution policies, ten run totals, adjudication notes, and final status—without publishing item-level material that could reconstruct the test.</p>
            <p>A repository-maintained schema defines that private record format. Actual records belong in the ignored <code>private/evaluations/</code> directory, outside <code>src/</code> and <code>public/</code>, so they are not bundled into client code or static output. No historical fields have been fabricated.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="level-6-beholder" aria-labelledby="beholder-title" data-level-status="Planned">
            <p class="section-label">Planned · reserved weight 32</p><h2 id="beholder-title">Level 6 — Beholder</h2>
            <p>Beholder has no active stimulus, task, or scoring protocol. It contributes nothing to the current Visual score, does not affect coverage or rank eligibility, and must not display a model score. Its future protocol must be documented before activation; no methodology is inferred here.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</main>
</Layout>
